d point, it must satisfy the conditions: $v_2(a_{p+1})=1$, $v_3(a_{p+1}) \\ge 1$, and $5 \\nmid a_{p+1}$.\n1.  $v_2(a_{p+1}) = v_2(a_1) - 2p = 1 \\implies v_2(a_1) = 2p+1$. Let $k=v_2(a_1)$. Then $k$ must be an odd integer $\\ge 3$ (since $p \\ge 1$). This satisfies $k \\ge 2p$.\n2.  $v_3(a_{p+1}) = v_3(a_1) - p \\ge 1 \\implies v_3(a_1) \\ge p+1$. Let $j=v_3(a_1)$. Substituting $p=(k-1)/2$, we get $j \\ge (k-1)/2+1 = (k+1)/2$. This satisfies $j \\ge p$.\n3.  For $a_{p+1}$ to be a fixed point, its prime factors other than 2 and 3 must be $\\ge 7$.\n    $a_{p+1} = \\frac{13^p}{2^{2p}3^p} a_1$. If $a_1 = 2^k 3^j m$, then $a_{p+1} = 2 \\cdot 3^{j-p} \\cdot 13^p \\cdot m$.\n    The prime factors of $a_{p+1}$ are $2, 3, 13$ and the prime factors of $m$. Since $13 \\ge 7$, this requires that all prime factors of $m$ must be $\\ge 7$. This also ensures that $5 \\nmid a_1$.\n\nThis gives the second family of solutions for $a_1$: $N = 2^k \\cdot 3^j \\cdot m$, where $k \\ge 3$ is an odd integer, $j \\ge (k+1)/2$, and all prime factors of $m$ are $\\ge 7$. For such $N$, $\\tau(N)=(k+1)(j+1)\\tau(m) \\ge 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1 = 12$, so they have at least three proper divisors.\n\nAny other starting value for $a_1$ leads to a sequence that eventually has a term that is odd, or has $v_2=1$ but is not a fixed point, or is not divisible by 3, all of which lead to termination.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe possible values of $a_1$ are the union of the two sets derived:\n1.  $N = 2 \\cdot 3^j \\cdot m$, where $j \\ge 1$ and $m$ is a positive integer whose prime factors are all $\\ge 7$.\n2.  $N = 2^k \\cdot 3^j \\cdot m$, where $k \\ge 3$ is an odd integer, $j \\ge (k+1)/2$, and $m$ is a positive integer whose prime factors are all $\\ge 7$."
