                    "text": "======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA proper divisor of a positive integer $N$ is a positive divisor of $N$ other than $N$ itself.\n\nThe infinite sequence $a_1,a_2,\\ldots$ consists of positive integers, each of which has at least three proper divisors. For each $n\\ge1$, the integer $a_{n+1}$ is the sum of three largest proper divisors of $a_n$.\n\nDetermine all possible values of $a_1$.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet $f(N)$ be the sum of the three largest proper divisors of $N$. The sequence is defined by $a_{n+1} = f(a_n)$ for $n \\ge 1$. Each $a_n$ must have at least three proper divisors, so its number of divisors $\\tau(a_n)$ must be at least 4.\n\nLet $1 = \\delta_1 < \\delta_2 < \\delta_3 < \\delta_4 < \\dots$ be the divisors of an integer $N$ in increasing order. The three largest proper divisors of $N$ are $N/\\delta_2, N/\\delta_3, N/\\delta_4$. Thus, $a_{n+1} = f(a_n) = a_n(1/\\delta_2 + 1/\\delta_3 + 1/\\delta_4)$.\n\nAn infinite sequence of positive integers $(a_n)$ cannot be strictly decreasing for all $n$ greater than some $n_0$. Therefore, for the sequence to be infinite, there must be infinitely many $n$ such that $a_{n+1} \\ge a_n$. This implies $1/\\delta_2 + 1/\\delta_3 + 1/\\delta_4 \\ge 1$. This inequality holds only if $\\delta_2=2$ and $\\delta_3=3$. Thus, there must be infinitely many terms in the sequence that are divisible by 6.\n\n**1. Necessary Conditions for Terms in an Infinite Sequence**\n\n**Lemma 1:** In an infinite sequence $(a_n)$, every term $a_n$ must be even.\n*Proof:* Suppose some term $a_k$ is odd. All its divisors are odd, so $a_{k+1} = f(a_k)$ is a sum of three odd integers, which is odd. By induction, all subsequent terms $a_n$ for $n \\ge k$ are odd. For any odd integer $N$, its smallest prime factor $p$ is at least 3. The largest proper divisor is $N/p \\le N/3$. Thus, $f(N) < 3(N/p) \\le N$. So, the subsequence $(a_n)_{n \\ge k}$ is strictly decreasing. A strictly decreasing sequence of positive integers must terminate, which contradicts the existence of an infinite sequence. Therefore, all terms $a_n$ must be even.\n\n**Lemma 2:** In an infinite sequence $(a_n)$, no term $a_n$ can be divisible by 5.\n*Proof:* Assume for contradiction that some term $a_k$ is divisible by 5. Since the sequence is infinite, there must be a term $a_m$ with $m \\ge k$ such that $a_{m+1} \\ge a_m$. This requires $a_m$ to be divisible by 6. Thus, if any term is divisible by 5, there must be a term divisible by $2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 = 30$. Let $a_n$ be such a term.\n1.  If $v_2(a_n)=1$, its smallest divisors are $1,2,3,5$. Then $a_{n+1} = a_n(1/2+1/3+1/5) = \\frac{31}{30}a_n$. Since $v_2(a_n)=1$, $v_2(a_{n+1}) = v_2(a_n) + v_2(31) - v_2(30) = 1+0-1=0$. So $a_{n+1}$ is odd. By Lemma 1, this leads to termination.\n2.  If $v_2(a_n) \\ge 2$, its smallest divisors are $1,2,3,4$. Then $a_{n+1} = a_n(1/2+1/3+1/4) = \\frac{13}{12}a_n$. Note that $v_2(a_{n+1}) = v_2(a_n)-2$ and $v_5(a_{n+1})=v_5(a_n)$. This transformation reduces the exponent of 2. This can only be applied as long as $v_2(a_n) \\ge 2$ and $v_3(a_n) \\ge 1$. Since the exponent of 2 decreases at each such step, this process must end. It ends when a term $a_N$ is produced with $v_2(a_N)<2$ or $v_3(a_N)<1$.\n    *   If $v_2(a_N)=1$ (and $v_3(a_N)\\ge 1, v_5(a_N)\\ge 1$), we are in case 1, which leads to termination.\n    *   If $v_2(a_N)=0$ or $v_3(a_N)=0$, the term is either odd or not divisible by 3. An odd term leads to termination. A term not divisible by 3 is part of a strictly decreasing subsequence (as shown in Lemma 3 below).\nAny term divisible by 5 is on a path that leads to termination. Thus, no term in an infinite sequence can be divisible by 5.\n\n**Lemma 3:** In an infinite sequence $(a_n)$, there exists an integer $N_0$ such that for all $n \\ge N_0$